{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/258289-iron-legion-is-calling-would-you-answer","id":258289,"title":"Iron Legion is calling! Would you answer?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-09-16T10:54:21-07:00","id":258289,"title":"Iron Legion is calling! Would you answer?","content":"When I first saw Darryl Elliot\u2019s illustration for the \u201cUniversal Soldier\u201d sample character in the \u201cHierarchy\u201d two things happened to me: Darryl Elliot became my second most favorite WtO illustrator (the first one will forever be John Cobb, hands down), and the Iron Legion \u2013 the most favorite Legion of Stygia.<br /> The concept of \u201cUniversal Soldier\u201d, a person, who have fought in countless battles, survived most horrific wars and, never the less, lived to an old age and died a more or less decent person, is, for me at least, a very captivating and interesting one. I, personally, hope to be enlisted in the Iron Legion <i>post mortem</i>, if enlisted at all <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> So this, and the most recent article on the badassoftheweek.com (and the appalling lack of any threads devoted to WtO20\u2026) got me thinking \u2013 what kind of <i>real</i> persons would make into the Iron Legions military branch?<br /> <br /> So, I ask you, my fellow forumites, with your examples. To qualify said individuals must: <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Die from the natural causes being well past 65 y.o. (we don\u2019t want Boney Hands and Grinning Face to argue over them, do we?)</li> <li>Have something to do with any branch of military \u2013 maybe even not the regular one, they might be mercenaries, explorers in the high age of colonialism or even the agents of various Intelligence Offices.</li> <li>To be on the safe side mataplot wise (just in case!) \u2013 they must be dead before 1999.</li> <li>Be a badass.</li> <li>The last, but not least \u2013 they should be not the \u201cfirst tier famous\u201d, like the world famous generals, kings, emperors and soldiers.</li> </ol><br /> <br /> <br /> From the badassoftheweek.com I\u2019d say the following people qualify:<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.badassoftheweek.com/index.cgi?id=36970514906\" target=\"_blank\">Frederick Albert &quot;Mike&quot; Mitchell-Hedges</a> \u2013 probably, the closest thing the real world had for Indiana Jones.<br /> - <a href=\"http://www.badassoftheweek.com/index.cgi?id=629549826684\" target=\"_blank\">Eddie Chapman</a> \u2013 An ex-criminal turned British spy who\u2019ve managed to win the trust of Wermacht.<br /> - <a href=\"http://www.badassoftheweek.com/index.cgi?id=140005029320\" target=\"_blank\">Samuel Whittemore</a> \u2013 a quintessential 18<sup>th</sup> century \u201cUniversal Soldier\u201d, who\u2019ve fought in nearly all Amrican conflicts of that era \u2013 even in the War of Independents. Aged 80.<br /> - <a href=\"http://www.badassoftheweek.com/index.cgi?id=30045813384\" target=\"_blank\">Adrian Carton de Wiart</a> \u201cwas a Belgian aristocrat who survived 11 wounds across three wars, led infantry charges on three continents, survived a couple plane crashes, was shot in the face at least three times, lost an eye, a hand, and a lung, accumulated enough shrapnel in his body to set off every metal detector from here to Berlin, made friends with basically every world leader from Pope Pius to Chiang Kai-Shek, and still spent the majority of his 80s hunting ducks one-handed on a massive estate where he lived with a woman half his age\u201d<br /> - <a href=\"http://www.badassoftheweek.com/index.cgi?id=79143016174\" target=\"_blank\">Paul Emil von Lettow-Vorbeck</a> \u201cA no-bullshit master of backcountry African bush warfare, Lettow-Vorbeck was not only a hero of Germany, but a man who to this day commands the respect of a worthy adversary from the South Africans \u2013 which is damn high praise. And, if that\u2019s not enough, after the war he was placed under Nazi surveillance because he personally told Hitler to \u201cgo fuck himself.\u201d<br /> - <a href=\"http://www.badassoftheweek.com/index.cgi?id=272031711290\" target=\"_blank\">Luis Alves de Lima e Silva, duke of Caxias</a> \u2013 the great war hero of the Brazillian Empire, among other things \u2013 personally lead a cavalry charge at the age of 64 and won the day.<br /> - <a href=\"http://badassoftheweek.com/index.cgi?ccpage=1&amp;ccid=54776576135\" target=\"_blank\">Jean Thurel</a> \u2013 a simple French soldier (later - NCO) who served three different monarchs, \u201cwas somehow engaged in roughly every French military engagement in the 18<sup>th</sup> century, and was so impossible to kill in battle that he managed to have his military career span nine decades\u201d.<br /> <br /> Personally, I\u2019d like to present one such qualifying figure, now largely forgotten both in Russia and the USA, despite the fact that this man\u2019s fate was tied to both of these countries.<br /> <br /> <b>Ivan</b><b> Vasilyevich Turchaninov (1822-1901) </b><br /> Don Cossack. Colonel General Staff. The hero of the American Civil War. Brigadier General<br /> <br /> The man who left his mark and his name in the history of the United States, was born in the village of Konstantinovskaia in the family of an officer, a nobleman of the Don Cossacks. He graduated from a military school in Novocherkassk. Then, however, went to study not in the Cavalry School, but in the metropolitan Mikhailovsky Artillery college, which he graduated from as an officer in 1841.<br /> Began his with the rank of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chor%C4%85%C5%BCy\" target=\"_blank\"><i>khorunzhij</i></a> in the 5th Don horse artillery battery. Then he was transferred to St. Petersburg, in the Guards horse artillery: <i>praporshik</i> (an ensign) in the Guards (6th) Don battery. Then he served in the Polish kingdom.<br /> Got his \u201cbaptism in fire\u201d in the event of the Hungarian campaign in 1849 under the banner of Field Marshal I.F. Paskevich. Then the Russian army saved the Austrian Habsburg monarchy from rebel against it in Hungary.<br /> Studied in Nicholaevsk General Staff Academy from 1849 to 1952. Here Ivan Turchaninov graduated at age 30 with a silver medal. He served in the 2nd Guards Infantry Division. Participated in the Crimean (or Eastern) War of 1853-1856. In its course was promoted to colonel of the General Staff.<br /> <br /> By virtue of his education and the rejection of the then still existing serfdom, Ivan considered it impossible for a further service in the Russian army and remaining in the homeland. In the spring of 1856 he went with his wife Nadezhda ostensibly for medical treatment abroad. In fact, the couple left Russia.<br /> Ivan Turchaninov became a supporter of the ideas of Herzen, which he promoted in his magazine published &quot;Kolokol&quot;. Colonel of the General Staff met with Herzen in London, then soon moved to permanent residence in the United States. Here Ivan Turchaninov became known as John Basil Turchin.<br /> But even there, there existed slavery, over which Cossack emigrant was appaled. He wrote in 1859 to Herzen in London, who\u2019ve previously told him that &quot;The land of America is boring&quot;:<br /> &quot;... I kind of doubt in your words, my dreams overseas were much higher and purer then the trite reality ... This republic - a paradise for the rich, they are truly independent; the most horrible crimes and darkest machinations are paid off with money ... &quot;<br /> Turchaninovs - Ivan and Nadezhda \u2013 managed to accommodate for the overseas life pretty soon. Already a former colonel of the General Staff in the Russian Army had finished engineering college in Philadelphia and began working as an engineer-surveyor in Chicago. The couple dreamed of returning to Europe. But the events in the United States changed their life plans.<br /> <br /> In the America the Civil War broke out which ultimately claimed 600 thousand lives.<br /> Turchaninov sided with the opponents of slavery. After just two months after the start of the rebellion in the slave states of the South, in June 1861, he was assigned as commander of the 19th Illinois Volunteer Regiment of the Union Army. He was allowed to retain the rank of colonel, which he had in Russia.<br /> Colonel John Basil Turchin, using the experience of the Russian army and Cossack troops, managed to achieve a high level of combat readiness in the entrusted to him by the American army regiment. Regiment initially was not involved in hostilities, while serving in the states of Missouri and Kentucky, which have not yet joined the rebellious Confederacy, but were hostile to the government in Washington.<br /> The direct part in the civil war this Russian emigrant took at the end of 1861, when he was appointed commander of the brigade, which consisted of four regiments. In May 1862 his brigade makes a successful raid from Tennessee to the south, in the state of Alabama. In that period pf war it was a notable success for northerners, which influenced the further course of the confrontation between North and South.<br /> However, according to the results of the raid on the enemy's rear Turchaninov had serious differences with the army command. Firstly, it is in the course of the operation he supplied his troops at the expense of the rebellious slave-owning planters. Second, he refused to give back from his camp runaway black slaves. In the latter case, he is likely to have acted according to the old Cossack law: <i>\u0441</i><i> \u0414\u043e\u043d\u0430</i><i> \u0432\u044b\u0434\u0430\u0447\u0438</i><i> \u043d\u0435\u0442</i><i>!</i> (&quot;No Extradition from Don!&quot;)<br /> In response, the authorities accused the army of Colonel John Basil Turchin in \u201cungentlemanly conduct\u201d, in the contempt for the individual and private property. According to American laws of the time, such allegations were very serious, and so he faced a court-martial. In those days, the newspapers of the North wrote about his behavior as follows:<br /> &quot;It is with courage he inspires all respect, he is full of military directness ... This is a man with a powerful physical composition and characteristic military demeanor, his face expresses the courage and intelligence. &quot;<br /> At trial Turchaninov pleaded guilty only of one thing: he allowed his wife to be with him in the war as a nurse. The court ruled to dismiss him from the ranks of the Union army. This decision has caused open resentment of his soldiers and officers who lost their beloved commander.<br /> Nadezhda Turchaninova personally appealed for help to the President of the United States Abraham Lincoln. He acted diplomatically without interfering in the judicial process, and promoted a Russian colonel to brigadier general. It was sign of direct approval of the presiden of his actionst. John Basil Turchin, freed from the investigation, remained in the ranks of the Union army.<br /> <br /> Turchaninov\u2019s brigade subsequently participated in persistent and bloody battles at Chikamote and Chattanooga. Brigadier-General has repeatedly demonstrated personal bravery. So, in September 1863 he personally led his soldiers in a bayonet charge.<br /> In November of the same year Turchaninov\\s regiments participated in the assault on the Confederate fortifications on Missionary Ridge near Chattanooga. In May 1864 the former officer of the Russian General Staff was one of the generals, who, under the banner of the Sherman made a victorious invasion of the southern state of Georgia.<br /> This campaign was the last one for Ivan Turchaninov in the Civil War in the United States. In the same 1864, Brigadier General was forced to leave the ranks of the Union army after suffering a hear attack.<br /> <br /> Turchaninov returned back to Chicago. In 1865 he started publishing a military-journalistic monthly. The next five years he worked at the patent office, then returned to the profession of engineer-surveyor. He took care of the Polish immigrants, participants of the last uprising in the Kingdom of Poland against the Russian Empire. Retired Brigadier General procured for 500 Polish families the land south of Chicago, where they founded the Polish town of Radom.<br /> Then Ivan Turchaninov engaged in farming, while working on a book about the Civil War in the United States. His family was often in need of funds, and so he had to make an extra funds by playing the violin. In the end, the farmer John Basil Turchin finally broke gone. But for all that the couple was happy in life, as only the people who truly love each other could be.<br /> <br /> He died in the state-owned hospital, and was buried in the military cemetery in Mount City, Illinois amongst many of those who were his subordinates and colleagues during his time as Brigadier General Turchin.<br /> After three years with him was buried his wife, a former nurse of the Union army, as per the special permission of the United States War Department. Nadezhda Turchaninova was known that once she took over command in the place of her sick husband.<br /> In the &quot;Dictionary of the American Civil War,&quot; 1962 edition, there is an entire article devoted to Nadeazda Turchina (Turchaninova). It ends with the following words:<br /> &quot;... With all the charm of a woman, she had the energy and self-confidence of a man.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","name":"von Lyttenburgh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3479_